To implement a more robust and interactive init system in Rust, named `sysx`, we'll enhance the previous code by adding a command-line interface (CLI) for managing tasks, ensuring the system can mount filesystems and start network interfaces. Additionally, we'll improve the structure and documentation to make the code more maintainable and understandable.

Below is the enhanced version of the `sysx` init program:

```rust
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;
use clap::Parser;

/// A simple init system written in Rust.
#[derive(Parser, Debug)]
struct Args {
    /// The command to run: 'start', 'stop', or 'status'.
    #[clap(subcommand)]
    cmd: Option<Commands>,
}

#[derive(Parser, Debug)]
enum Commands {
    /// Start a service.
    Start { service: String },
    /// Stop a service.
    Stop { service: String },
    /// Check the status of a service.
    Status { service: String },
}

// Define a struct to hold the name and command of each service.
struct Service {
    name: String,
    command: String,
    status: bool,
}

impl Service {
    fn new(name: &str, command: &str) -> Service {
        Service {
            name: name.to_string(),
            command: command.to_string(),
            status: false,
        }
    }

    // Method to start the service.
    fn start(&mut self) {
        println!("Starting service: {}", self.name);
        let mut child = Command::new("sh")
            .arg("-c")
            .arg(&self.command)
            .stdout(Stdio::piped())
            .spawn()
            .expect("Failed to start service");

        // In a real scenario, we would handle the process differently.
        // Here, we just wait for it to finish.
        let _output = child.wait_with_output().expect("Failed to wait on child");
        self.status = true;
    }

    // Method to stop the service.
    fn stop(&mut self) {
        println!("Stopping service: {}", self.name);
        self.status = false;
    }

    // Method to check the status of the service.
    fn status(&self) {
        if self.status {
            println!("{} is running.", self.name);
        } else {
            println!("{} is stopped.", self.name);
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let args = Args::parse();

    // Define a list of basic services to start.
    let mut services = vec![
        Service::new("mount /proc", "mount -t proc proc /proc"),
        Service::new("mount /sys", "mount -t sysfs sysfs /sys"),
        Service::new("network", "ifup eth0"),
        Service::new("cron", "cron -f"),
        Service::new("sshd", "sshd -D"),
    ];

    match &args.cmd {
        Some(Commands::Start { service }) => {
            if let Some(svc) = services.iter_mut().find(|svc| svc.name == *service) {
                svc.start();
            } else {
                eprintln!("Service {} not found.", service);
            }
        }
        Some(Commands::Stop { service }) => {
            if let Some(svc) = services.iter_mut().find(|svc| svc.name == *service) {
                svc.stop();
            } else {
                eprintln!("Service {} not found.", service);
            }
        }
        Some(Commands::Status { service }) => {
            if let Some(svc) = services.iter().find(|svc| svc.name == *service) {
                svc.status();
            } else {
                eprintln!("Service {} not found.", service);
            }
        }
        None => {
            // If no command is provided, start all services.
            for service in &mut services {
                service.start();
            }
        }
    }

    // Simulate keeping the init process alive.
    loop {
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(60));
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
- **Command-Line Interface**: We've used the `clap` crate to parse command-line arguments. This allows users to interact with the init system using commands like `start`, `stop`, and `status`.
- **Service Management**: Each service now has a `status` field indicating whether the service is running. The `start`, `stop`, and `status` methods manage the service state accordingly.
- **Service List**: A list of services including filesystems (`/proc`, `/sys`), network, and other services is defined at the beginning of the `main` function. These services can be started, stopped, or checked for their status via the command line.
- **Main Loop**: The program enters an infinite loop after processing commands to simulate keeping the init system alive.

